1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,046
If your hair's turning gray way earlier than your friends', it's not always genetics - three things can speed it up.

2
00:00:05,366 --> 00:00:11,639
Hair gets its color from melanocytes, pigment cells that need vitamin B12, zinc, and copper to keep working.

3
00:00:11,959 --> 00:00:15,209
Once one of those runs short, gray strands show up faster.

4
00:00:15,529 --> 00:00:27,897
First, low B12, common on a plant-based diet - one study found over half of people with premature gray hair had low B12, and since it mainly comes from meat, eggs, and dairy, vegans run a higher risk.

5
00:00:28,217 --> 00:00:30,152
Second, overdoing zinc supplements.

6
00:00:30,472 --> 00:00:39,477
The NIH caps daily zinc at 40 milligrams, but around 60 milligrams for ten weeks can block copper absorption, and copper is what activates the enzyme that makes melanin.

7
00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:40,973
Third, chronic stress.

8
00:00:41,293 --> 00:00:48,919
A Harvard study found stress floods hair follicles with norepinephrine, burning through the stem cells that produce pigment - once that's gone, gray doesn't come back.

9
00:00:49,239 --> 00:00:52,527
If you're plant-based, add a B12 supplement or fortified foods.

10
00:00:52,847 --> 00:01:01,927
If you take zinc, check the label so you're not stacking a multivitamin, lozenges, and fortified snacks at once, and pair food-based zinc with copper-rich cashews or leafy greens.

11
00:01:02,247 --> 00:01:06,914
And build in real stress relief, like sleep and exercise, before it catches up with your hair.
